genetically modified organisms are back  in the news as EU environment ministers  meet in Brussels to debate the  possibility for member states to  restrict or even prohibit their  cultivation this could apply in all or  part of the territory to GMOs that have  been authorized at EU level for reasons  other than health and environmental  considerations while they're widely  grown in the Americas and Asia  genetically modified crops are generally  unpopular in Europe where public  opposition is strong and  environmentalist to raise concerns about  the impact on biodiversity ministers  will also discuss the new framework for  climate and energy policy for 2030 the  main elements are a reduction in  greenhouse gas emissions by forty  percent below the 1990 level and an  eu-wide binding target for renewable  energy of at least twenty seven percent  energy efficiency is a key component of  the 2030 framework and a review of the  energy efficiency directive is expected  to be concluded later this year  ministers will also hold a debate on the  annual growth survey notably on how to  improve the uptake of green economy  priorities into the national reform  programmes of member states and in the  overall semester cycle
 

